Your project references the MSTest 3.8.2 metapackage on net9.0, so constructor injection of `TestContext` (added in 3.6) is available. Split setup by whether it is synchronous or asynchronous:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class RepositoryTests
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;
    private readonly FakeDatabase _db;

    public RepositoryTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;
        _db = new FakeDatabase();     // synchronous setup belongs in the constructor
    }

    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task InitializeAsync()
    {
        await _db.SeedAsync();        // async-only setup
    }

    [TestCleanup]
    public void Cleanup() => _db.Reset();
}
```

The recommended shape:

- Do all synchronous initialization in the constructor. That lets the fields be `readonly` and guarantees they are assigned, so the nullability analyzer is satisfied without `= null!` or a `?` annotation.
- Inject `TestContext` as a constructor parameter rather than a settable property, since you are on 3.6+. It stays a `readonly` field.
- Use `[TestInitialize]` only for the async part, because a constructor cannot be `async`.
- Put anything that must run even when the test fails in `[TestCleanup]`.
- Seal the class.

Execution order per test with constructor injection is: constructor (receives `TestContext`) → `[TestInitialize]` → test method → `[TestCleanup]` → `DisposeAsync` → `Dispose`. If you instead kept a `TestContext` property, MSTest sets it after the constructor runs (constructor → set `TestContext` property → `[TestInitialize]`), and in that case you should not add a nullable annotation or `= null!` to the property.
